"""Small mechanisms and explanations of what their results establish."""
CASE_FOR_UNIT={2: 5, 3: 1, 4: 12, 5: 9, 6: 4, 7: 8, 8: 9, 9: 12, 10: 8, 11: 5, 12: 1, 13: 12, 14: 5, 15: 10, 16: 8, 17: 9, 18: 12, 19: 9, 20: 1, 21: 1}
CODE={
2: ('Python · examples/mechanisms.py', 'from mechanisms import capacity, amdahl\nprint(capacity())\nprint(amdahl(.8, 4))', 'The defaults produce about 5,787.04 average requests/s and 28,935.19 at the assumed peak. Dividing by 600×0.6 and rounding up gives 81 active instances; allowing one to fail gives 82.\n\nThe worksheet sizes only its assumed request-serving resource. Database, image delivery, and the chosen failure placement still need their own capacity tests.'),
3: ('Python · arithmetic model', 'cold = dict(dns=20, tcp=40, tls=40, request_and_work=55)\nassert sum(cold.values()) == 155\nwarm = dict(request_and_work=55)\nassert sum(warm.values()) == 55', 'The cold path totals 155 ms. The warm model reuses the assumed DNS, TCP, and TLS setup, leaving the same 55 ms request/work stage.\n\nReal reuse depends on cache, protocol, connection state, and overlap. This code performs arithmetic; it does not open a connection or measure a protocol.'),
4: ('Pseudocode · bounded upload ownership', 'queue = bounded_queue(capacity=100)\nstart_workers(count=50)\nfor upload in arrivals:\n    reference = persist_upload_or_reject(upload)\n    queue.put_with_deadline(reference)\nworker():\n    while reference = queue.take():\n        try: process(reference)\n        finally: release_owned_resources()', 'The queue holds at most one hundred waiting references, and fifty workers limit active jobs. The large bytes remain in storage.\n\nA failed handoff still needs an explicit durable-job or rejection policy. A process-local queue alone cannot promise to resume accepted work after its process disappears.'),
5: ('Python · injected collaborator', 'class RecordingGateway:\n    def __init__(self): self.calls = []\n    def charge(self, command):\n        self.calls.append(command)\n        return "uncertain"\n\ndef pay(command, gateway):\n    return gateway.charge(command)\n\ngateway = RecordingGateway()\nassert pay({"amount_minor":100,"currency":"USD"}, gateway) == "uncertain"', 'The recording gateway receives the command and returns uncertainty. Passing it as an argument makes this behaviour easy to exercise without a real provider.\n\nA complete payment service also checks money, persists progress, and reconciles outcomes. This small example isolates the dependency; it does not implement those other obligations.'),
6: ('Python · lifetime identities', 'singleton = object()\ndef new_scope(): return {"scoped": object()}\na, b = new_scope(), new_scope()\nassert a["scoped"] is a["scoped"]\nassert a["scoped"] is not b["scoped"]\nassert object() is not object()\nassert singleton is singleton', 'Within a scope, the same scoped identity is reused. Different scopes have different objects, while the process-owned singleton stays the same.\n\nThis models identity only. An actual framework also defines registration and disposal. A request-owned resource cannot safely become a retained singleton dependency just because a reference remains available.'),
7: ('Pseudocode · module contract', "place_order(command):\n    priced = catalog.quote(command.items)\n    reservation = inventory.reserve(command.items)\n    payment = payments.authorize(command.operation_id, priced.total)\n    return orders.record_outcomes(priced, reservation, payment)\n# Modules expose contracts; they do not update each other's private tables.", "The modules communicate through their operations rather than writing each other's private tables. Their results let the caller record what actually happened.\n\nCalling three remote services in this order does not create one atomic transaction. Some local changes can share a deliberate transaction, while external effects need a workflow and recovery rules."),
8: ('Python · keyset continuation', 'from mechanisms import keyset_page\nrows = [(1,1,"A"), (1,2,"B"), (2,3,"C")]\nassert keyset_page(rows, after=(1,1)) == rows[1:]', 'The ordered time/ID pair gives equal timestamps a stable tie-breaker. Continuing after (1,1) returns the later rows in this fixed fixture.\n\nA real cursor also needs validation, query and tenant scope, and a rule for changes between pages. Keyset continuation does not itself freeze a changing dataset.'),
9: ('Pseudocode · bytes and metadata', 'upload = create_authorized_upload(tenant, expected_size)\nobject = store_and_verify_bytes(upload)\nwith transaction():\n    insert_document(tenant, object.version, object.digest)\n    insert_processing_intent(object.version)\n# Orphan-byte cleanup handles incomplete metadata handoff.', 'The metadata points to a verified immutable object version and records the processing obligation. If metadata commit fails, the object can remain orphaned and needs reconciliation or cleanup.\n\nThe local transaction does not include the earlier separate object upload. Track that handoff explicitly instead of describing it as one cross-service commit.'),
10: ('Python · conditional seat claim', 'from mechanisms import open_demo_store, claim_seat\ndb = open_demo_store()\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO seats VALUES (1,NULL)")\ndb.commit()\nassert claim_seat(db, 1, "A")\nassert not claim_seat(db, 1, "B")\ndb.close()', "A claims the available seat; B's later conditional claim fails. The affected-row result determines whether the change occurred. The executable checks also use two connections to the same store.\n\nThis protects a local ownership rule. Payment, hold expiry, and stale callbacks need connected but separate workflow logic."),
11: ('Python · ideal LRU', 'from mechanisms import LRU\ncache = LRU(3)\nfor key in "ABC": cache.put(key, key)\ncache.get("A")\nassert cache.put("D", "D") == ("B", "B")', 'After A,B,C,A, ideal LRU considers B the least recently used. Adding D evicts B, as the assertion checks.\n\nThe fixture models entry count and exact recency. Actual caches can approximate the policy and use byte or expiry limits. Eviction alone says nothing about whether a loaded value is current.'),
12: ('Python · stable rendezvous mapping', 'from mechanisms import rendezvous\nfor key in range(1000):\n    old = rendezvous(key, ["A","B","C"])\n    new = rendezvous(key, ["A","B","C","D"])\n    assert old == new or new == "D"', 'For each key, adding D either preserves the old owner or assigns the key to D. The assertion checks that stable-mapping property across the fixture.\n\nIt does not move stored data or synchronise client membership. A popular key still has one selected owner in this model, regardless of how balanced the key counts look.'),
13: ('Python · source and attempt fencing', 'from mechanisms import Workflow\nrun = Workflow("file-version-2", epoch=8)\nassert not run.complete(7, "file-version-2", "old")\nassert not run.complete(8, "file-version-1", "wrong-input")\nassert run.complete(8, "file-version-2", "current")', "The workflow rejects an old epoch and the wrong input version, then accepts the current combination. These local conditions make the attempt's authority explicit.\n\nA real store must check and apply them atomically and validate the output. Provenance also needs the transform version and relevant source evidence, not just a result filename."),
14: ('Python · ordering models', 'from mechanisms import vector_relation, lamport_receive\nassert vector_relation([1,0], [0,1]) == "concurrent"\nassert vector_relation([1,0], [1,2]) == "before"\nassert lamport_receive(5, 9) == 10', 'Neither [1,0] nor [0,1] dominates the other, so the model reports concurrency. [1,0] precedes [1,2], and receiving 9 at Lamport value 5 advances it to 10.\n\nThese are ordering calculations. A smaller Lamport number alone does not prove causation, and neither clock decides a business merge or consensus outcome.'),
15: ('Pseudocode · three replicas, one partition', 'A,B,C contain committed index 8\npartition isolates old leader A\nB and C elect eligible B in term 5\nB appends index 9, term 5\nB and C durably replicate index 9\nRaft commit rule establishes committed prefix\nA cannot acknowledge a new quorum-committed write alone\nrejoin: A follows term 5 and repairs uncommitted suffix', "The example assumes correct non-Byzantine participants, durable protocol state, and three fixed voters. B's current-term entry reaches a valid majority while A is isolated.\n\nFollow the full Raft rules for election, log repair, commitment, reads, and membership. This trace explains one history and is not an executable replacement protocol."),
16: ('Python · duplicate delivery, one local effect', 'from mechanisms import open_demo_store, consume_local_event\ndb = open_demo_store()\nassert consume_local_event(db, "E1", "orders")\nassert not consume_local_event(db, "E1", "orders")\nassert db.execute("SELECT value FROM counters").fetchone()[0] == 1\ndb.close()', 'The first transaction records E1 and increments the counter. The second finds E1 already processed and leaves the counter at one.\n\nEvent recognition and the local effect share a transaction. Replacing the counter update with an external email or payment call requires another protocol; it changes the boundary this example protects.'),
17: ('Python · retry amplification', 'attempts_per_layer = 3\nindependent_retry_layers = 4\nassert attempts_per_layer ** independent_retry_layers == 81', 'The arithmetic gives 81 permitted downstream attempts in the nested worst case. Actual execution can stop sooner, but failures may activate many layers at once.\n\nUse an overall retry budget and deadline, protect useful work, and keep operation identity stable. Jitter spreads attempt timing without reducing the configured total by itself.'),
18: ('Python · synthetic tenant policy', 'from mechanisms import permit_demo\nactor = {"tenant":"A", "active":True, "actions":["read"]}\nassert permit_demo(actor, {"tenant":"A"}, "read")\nassert not permit_demo(actor, {"tenant":"B"}, "read")', 'The predicate permits the same-tenant read and rejects tenant A reading tenant B. It assumes the supplied actor and resource attributes are already trustworthy.\n\nThis is authorisation logic, not credential validation. Every object, queue, cache, and export path must enforce the relevant rule with valid context.'),
19: ('Python · one trace accounting model', 'stages_ms = {"other":300, "pool_wait":4600, "query":100}\nassert sum(stages_ms.values()) == 5000\nassert stages_ms["pool_wait"] / sum(stages_ms.values()) == .92', "Pool acquisition takes 4,600 of 5,000 ms, or 92% of this request's elapsed time. The query itself accounts for only 100 ms.\n\nInvestigate hold times, locks, and the wider population before attributing the whole incident to one trace. The arithmetic locates the wait but does not establish why the pool is full."),
20: ('Pseudocode · safe release progression', 'add_compatible_schema_field()\ndeploy_mixed_version_safe_code()\nbackfill_with_bounded_progress()\nverify_old_and_new_readers()\nretire_old_readers()\nremove_obsolete_schema_only_when_safe()', 'Old and new readers coexist during compatible expansion and backfill. Retire old readers before removing the structures they need.\n\nRollback is safe only while its state and protocol assumptions still hold. Destructive contraction or external effects may require forward repair. Verify the actual running artifact and configuration too.'),
21: ('Python · fictional comparison', 'from mechanisms import cost_options\ntotals = {name:sum(parts.values()) for name,parts in cost_options().items()}\nassert totals == {"single":371, "replicated_app":551, "partitioned":1402}', 'The monthly totals are 371, 551, and 1,402 fictional units. They show how component costs add, rather than how the deployments actually perform.\n\nCheck equal requirements before ranking price. The additional latency and availability models have explicit synthetic inputs; real capacity, tails, and recovery require corresponding tests.'),
}
